from pathlib import Path
import json, textwrap
root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
rows=[
('Foundations','Why does data quality come first?','Poor definitions, biased samples, and inaccurate measurements undermine conclusions, even with advanced analysis.'),
('DCOVA','What are the five DCOVA tasks?','Define, Collect, Organize, Visualize, Analyze. Establish the business objective before applying these tasks.'),
('Operational definitions','What makes a variable operationally defined?','Specify exactly what it measures, the observational unit, units, time period, and inclusion rules.'),
('Variables','What is a variable?','A characteristic that takes different values across observations. An observational unit is the person or item measured.'),
('Variable types','How do categorical and numerical variables differ?','Categorical values identify groups. Numerical values express quantities for which arithmetic has a meaningful interpretation.'),
('Discrete and continuous','Counts or measurements?','Discrete variables have countable values, such as purchases. Continuous variables can take any value in an interval, such as elapsed time.'),
('Recording choices','Can the same concept have different variable types?','Yes. Age in years is numerical. Age groups are categorical. Classification depends on the operational definition.'),
('Nominal scale','What information does a nominal scale provide?','Categories without an inherent order. Examples: payment method and product category. Numeric codes remain labels.'),
('Ordinal scale','Why are satisfaction ratings ordinal?','Categories have an order, but equal distances between adjacent categories are not established.'),
('Interval scale','What distinguishes an interval scale?','Equal differences are meaningful, but zero is arbitrary. A 30 C temperature is 10 C above 20 C, without being 1.5 times as hot.'),
('Ratio scale','When are ratios meaningful?','With equal intervals and a meaningful zero. A 20-minute duration is twice a 10-minute duration.'),
('Measurement overview','How do the four scales build on one another?','Nominal: categories. Ordinal: order too. Interval: meaningful differences too. Ratio: meaningful ratios too.'),
('Method choice','Does measurement scale alone determine the analysis?','No. Also consider the objective, research design, sampling structure, distribution, and assumptions.'),
('Primary and secondary','What distinguishes primary from secondary data?','Primary data are collected for the current purpose. Secondary data were collected previously for another purpose. The distinction depends on the user and purpose.'),
('Data sources','What five data sources does the guide identify?','Distributed sources, designed experiments, surveys, observational studies, and ongoing business activities.'),
('Experiments','Why does random assignment matter?','It helps create comparable treatment groups and supports causal inference when the experiment is properly designed.'),
('Observation and surveys','What can observation and surveys establish?','Observation records behavior without assigned treatments. Surveys collect reported responses. Association alone does not establish causation.'),
('Population and sample','How do a population and sample differ?','The population is the full group of interest. A sample is a selected subset used to learn about that group.'),
('Parameter and statistic','Which describes a population, and which a sample?','A parameter describes the population. A statistic summarizes the sample and may estimate a parameter under suitable design and assumptions.'),
('Census','Does a census eliminate every source of error?','A complete census has no sampling error. Coverage, nonresponse, and measurement errors may still occur.'),
('Data structure','What are structured, unstructured, and semistructured data?','Structured: fixed rows and fields. Unstructured: free-form text or media. Semistructured: some organization with flexible content.'),
('Data cleaning','What should an analyst check before analysis?','Missing values, impossible values, invalid categories, inconsistent formats, duplicate records, and unusual observations. Document decisions.'),
('Outliers','Should an unusual observation always be deleted?','No. Investigate whether it is an error or a valid extreme value. Preserve the raw record and document any correction or exclusion.'),
('Recoding','What makes grouped categories valid?','Mutually exclusive: no overlap. Collectively exhaustive: every eligible value fits. State boundaries and missing-value handling.'),
('Sampling frame','What is a sampling frame?','The list or mechanism from which units are selected. Compare it with the target population to detect omissions or ineligible entries.'),
('Probability sampling','What defines a probability sample?','Each population unit has a known, positive selection probability under the design. Probabilities need not be equal.'),
('Nonprobability sampling','How do convenience and judgment samples differ?','Convenience uses accessible participants. Judgment uses researcher expertise. Unknown selection probabilities limit design-based population inference.'),
('Simple random sampling','What defines a simple random sample of fixed size?','Every possible sample of that size has the same selection probability. A complete frame is usually needed.'),
('Replacement','What changes when sampling with replacement?','A selected unit can appear again. Without replacement, selected units cannot be selected a second time.'),
('Systematic sampling','How would you sample 200 of 10,000 items?','For this divisible frame, k = N/n = 50. Choose a random start from 1 to 50, then select every 50th item.'),
('Systematic limitations','What threatens a systematic sample?','Periodicity aligned with the sampling interval can produce an unrepresentative sample. Check the ordering before selection.'),
('Stratified sampling','How does stratified sampling select units?','Divide the population into nonoverlapping strata and randomly sample within every stratum. This supports subgroup representation.'),
('Sampling allocation','What if a small stratum is oversampled?','Its sample share exceeds its population share. Use design-appropriate weights when estimating overall population quantities.'),
('Cluster sampling','How does one-stage cluster sampling work?','Randomly select some natural groups, then observe all eligible units in those groups. It can reduce travel and fieldwork costs.'),
('Two-stage sampling','What is two-stage cluster sampling?','First randomly select clusters. Then randomly sample units within the selected clusters.'),
('Strata and clusters','What is the central distinction?','Stratified: sample units from every group. Cluster: select some groups. Similar units within clusters can reduce precision.'),
('Survey errors','What four survey errors should you assess?','Coverage, nonresponse, sampling, and measurement. Several can occur in the same survey.'),
('Coverage','What is coverage error?','The frame fails to match the target population, for example when a survey of all students uses a full-time-only list.'),
('Nonresponse','When can nonresponse create bias?','When missing respondents differ systematically on the quantity of interest. A response rate alone does not determine the size of the bias.'),
('Sampling error','Will increasing sample size remove bias?','Larger well-designed samples usually reduce random sampling variation. They do not automatically remove coverage, nonresponse, or measurement bias.'),
('Measurement error','What question features can distort responses?','Leading wording, ambiguity, double-barreled questions, unclear reference periods, recall difficulties, and interviewer influence.'),
('Neutral wording','How can a leading satisfaction question be improved?','Ask how satisfied or dissatisfied the respondent was with the service during the most recent visit. Avoid praising the service in the question.'),
('Survey ethics','What does honest survey reporting require?','Disclose the target population, frame, selection method, sample size, response limitations, wording, and uncertainty. Avoid manipulating selection or interpretation.'),
('Literary Digest','What is the key lesson of the 1936 poll example?','A very large response count cannot guarantee representativeness. Frame coverage and differential nonresponse both matter.'),
('GT&M acquisition','What evidence supports an acquisition decision?','Consistently defined sales and expenses, economic context, and relevant customer data. Extra profitability and risk measures are teaching extensions, not a calculated valuation.'),
('Whitney Wireless','How should a best-sales-year claim be checked?','Request comparable annual revenue and unit sales across all relevant years, with clear scales, accounting definitions, and reporting periods.'),
('Ashland MultiComm','Does falling renewal prove prices are too high?','No. Examine subscriber counts, revenue, and renewal rates. Price, competition, service, and streaming are possible explanations to investigate.'),
('CardioGood Fitness','How should its customer variables be classified?','Model: nominal. Self-rated fitness 1-5: ordinal. Income and age: numerical. Weekly sessions: discrete counts. Distance: continuous measurement.'),
('Clear Mountain surveys','Can 62 undergraduate and 44 graduate responses prove representativeness?','No. Assess selection, frame, and nonresponse. The separate questionnaires and datasets were not supplied, so their exact variables cannot be confirmed.'),
('Integrated review','A frame omits part-time students, 400 of 1,000 reply, and wording is leading. Which errors apply?','Coverage from omitted students. Potential nonresponse bias from missing replies. Sampling variation from selection. Measurement error from leading wording.')]
cards=[]
for topic,q,a in rows:
 cards.append(dict(topic=topic,question='\n'.join(textwrap.wrap(q,48)),answer='\n'.join(textwrap.wrap(a,53)),cue='Recall the distinction and explain why it matters.',lesson='1',source='chapter01-original.txt'))
(root/'book-chapters/basic-business-statistics-13e/chapter01-cards.json').write_text(json.dumps(cards,indent=2)+'\n')
print(len(cards),'cards')
